# The suites apt.fleetless.dev serves: one per ROS 2 distribution this package
# is built for, named after that distribution.
#
# **The suite name is the guarantee.** A robot's sources.list line names one
# suite, and each suite carries only its own distribution's package, so a Humble
# robot never sees `ros-jazzy-fleetless-bridge` at all -- apt answers `Unable to
# locate package` rather than offering it. That is not a convention somebody
# remembers at publish time: `apt/publish-deb.sh` derives the suite from the
# .deb's own `Package:` field and refuses a mismatch, and
# `apt/verify-suites.sh` builds a throwaway repository from this exact file and
# proves the refusal by running `apt-get install` for the other two packages in
# each distribution's own container.
#
# **These stanzas must be exactly the distributions in `tools/distros.sh`** --
# no more, no fewer. `test/test_apt_suites.py` asserts that against the table,
# because a suite here with no table entry can never be built for, and a table
# entry with no suite here builds a .deb that has nowhere to go. Adding a
# distribution is: one entry in `tools/distros.sh`, one stanza here, one row in
# `README.md`'s support table.
#
# `Architectures: amd64 arm64` with an `Architecture: all` package is
# deliberate: reprepro files an arch-all .deb into every architecture listed
# here, so one build serves both. Dropping an architecture from a stanza does
# not fail a publish -- it silently stops serving that architecture from that
# suite, which is why the three stanzas are asserted identical apart from the
# codename and the description.
#
# `SignWith:` names the key by FINGERPRINT and not by uid. A uid-shaped lookup
# can match more than one key -- a retired key left in the same keyring carries
# a byte-identical uid -- and then signing picks one for you, silently and
# successfully. Forty hex digits cannot do that.

